WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.540
you can tell we are getting very close to
an election because a lot of the audience

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.780
questions coming in relate to the either the
democratic primary or the eventual general

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.360
election in November of 2020.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.940
So let's dig right into it.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:12.940
First question.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:17.820
Hey, David is the [inaudible] [inaudible]
strategy to split the democratic primary vote

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:24.260
as much as possible to force a brokered convention
and let super delegates decide on the second

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.260
ballot.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.300
So let's, we first have to explain this entire
thing.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.820
So you might remember back in 2016 as the
primaries were going on, you would see these

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.320
estimates of how many delegates each candidate
had.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.960
And very often you would see, for example,
Hillary Clinton and Bernie Sanders with like

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:50.150
the same number or close to the same number
of delegates on the basis of polling and thus

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.700
expected voting in the primary States.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.670
But then you would see another, which says
plus super delegates and Hillary had like

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:04.239
all the superdelegates and then it would seem
as though Bernie simply had no chance whatsoever

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.170
of securing the nomination.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:11.030
Even if you ran the table in the primaries
from that point forward.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:17.020
And this is one of the ways in which people
accurately said the DNC was set up to deny

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.539
Bernie the nomination in 2016 and to help
Hillary Clinton, which is that even before

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:27.649
any ballots were cast in primaries by voters,
super delegates were already known and expected

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:30.869
to be supporting mostly Hillary Clinton over
Bernie Sanders.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:37.310
So one of the things that the DNC did since
the last primary in order to at least appear

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:42.270
as though they're trying to make the primary
more democratic, as they said, super delegates

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.670
will not vote on the first ballot at the convention.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.229
So before the way it worked is you would have
all the primaries leading up to the democratic

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:51.969
national convention.

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:56.709
The primaries would lead to delegates being
sent to the DNC.

00:01:56.709 --> 00:02:00.869
The superdelegates would be there and they
would say, let's now vote, and the delegates

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:05.389
would vote based on how their state primary
went, and then the superdelegates would vote.

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:10.610
You add them all up and then somebody, the
nominee maybe or not you go to a second round

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:15.560
with the DNC, decided to do, as they said
in 2020 superdelegates will not vote on the

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.740
first round.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:23.390
Therefore, if a candidate has enough support
in the first round of voting, based solely

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.240
on the delegates from the primaries, the superdelegates
won't even get involved.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:34.460
But if after the first round of voting there
is not a nominee on the basis of the rules,

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.030
then we do a second round and the super delegates
are then allowed to participate.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.720
So if one strategy was we need to appear as
though we are putting our thumb on the scales

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.070
less than in 2016 but we still want the opportunity
to do that.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:53.300
You would say, all right, let's get rid of
superdelegate voting in round one, push superdelegate

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.261
voting to round two but let's try to make
it so that we get to that second round, make

00:02:58.261 --> 00:03:02.500
it so that there is not a candidate selected
in round one of voting at the DNC.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.870
And one way to do that is indeed to split
the vote, but it's actually a two tiered strategy.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.440
The strategy you're talking about of splitting
the vote in order to get super delegates in

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.620
a position to decide on the second ballot.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.820
There's a prior strategy to that, which is
let's just reinforce the establishment so

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.030
that Joe Biden or increasingly Pete Buddha
judge a win.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:32.170
And I actually was on TV last week talking
about this on the rising on rising the Hill,

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.600
the Hills rising, the show with crystal ball
and cigar and jetty.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.760
And I explained that even if it's not a carefully
crafted strategy, if the DNC is worried about

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.990
Biden because they see that he looks slow
during the debate, during the debates, they

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.910
see that he's not making a lot of sense on
some issues that every debate he drops in

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.650
polling the debate being more publicly visible
is worse for Biden.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.260
If they're worried about that, they can say
who's a good alternative.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.260
It's not Bernie.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:02.560
Uh, it's not Warren, it's Buddha, judge Buddha
judges the other establishment candidate that

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.670
we could get behind by supporting Pete Buddha
judge by giving him more airtime, uh, speaking

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:13.800
time during the MSNBC debate for example,
they are pulling more people into the universe

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.320
of centrism.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.260
And if in the end those voters say, you know
what, I don't actually want to vote for Buddha

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.979
judge cause he's not experienced enough.

00:04:20.979 --> 00:04:25.139
It's a very natural transition to instead
go and vote for Joe Biden.

00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:30.770
And so the initial strategy is to basically
default this thing to Joe Biden, but with

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:35.449
the understanding that Biden's having some
problems start supporting Buddha judge just

00:04:35.449 --> 00:04:42.550
to get more people thinking centrist rather
than leftist like Warren or Bernie and hopefully

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:47.330
they will just straight out win the nomination
in the first ballot on the basis of primary

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.330
results.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:54.020
Of course, if it doesn't happen, the strategy
of splitting the vote is an effective one

00:04:54.020 --> 00:05:00.879
to try to eventually let superdelegates decide
on round two in the democratic national convention.

00:05:00.879 --> 00:05:02.719
So they're doing it all.

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:06.949
It's a different strategy than in 2016 because
the rules have changed, but certainly don't

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:08.250
be confused for a second.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:14.400
The establishment knows who they want and
knows who they don't want and there are multiple

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.069
strategies happening at the same time to try
to make that a reality.

